      "title": "Read Maryland’s interest and notice rules carefully",
      "narration": "Maryland's modern policy-loan interest provision permits either the specified fixed maximum or an adjustable maximum calculated under the statute. Do not call six percent the universal cap for every policy loan. The contract must identify its applicable rate mechanism. Adjustable-rate loans have notice and adjustment rules. The standard loan provision also addresses termination when total debt reaches the loan value, including at least thirty days' notice to the specified parties. A loan for paying a premium is treated differently from other loans under the six-month deferral provision. Identify the transaction and controlling section before choosing a deadline or interest rule.",
